  "title": "The knot's junction is a similitude: unbranched twists count in polynomial time",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "Let B be a cubic bipartite Pfaffian graph (a three-fold space's incidence graph) with any signing sigma. (1) #col(B, sigma) = (3/2)^n times the sum over even subgraphs chi of (-1/3)^(|chi|/2) (-1)^|sigma & chi|; in the Hadamard basis points are EVEN3, lines [3,0,-1,0] and negations Z; flipping one incidence of a non-colourable system gives #col(H - L). (2) Every branch tensor at a trisum square is even in the Hadamard basis, and a planar branch gives a matchgate signature (the 4-ary matchgate identity in cyclic order). (3) An untwisted closed branch (some square pattern makes it Pfaffian) has the split form, and twistedness is inherited by larger branches. (4) Similitude: for an untwisted closed branch with split (alpha, beta), the junction maps every even 4-ary tensor F of the other closed branch to a tensor W with MGI(H W) = +-(alpha^2 + alpha beta + beta^2) MGI(H F), the sign set by the host's and the other branch's incidence signs; alpha^2 + alpha beta + beta^2 is the norm of alpha + beta e^(i pi/3). (5) If no square of the trisum tree has all three branches twisted, some leaf sees at most one twisted closed branch at every square, every junction is then a matchgate signature, and #col(B, sigma) is computable in polynomial time. (6) Junctions of two twisted closed branches can leave the matchgate class.",
